{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"ace2a2969fff14699","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Cxbwokq1XMvJjn87kQykrf","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":89030,"tool_uses":21,"duration_ms":171874},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"8d217eca-377d-4adf-bcec-939894b7c46a","session_id":"6cf1809e-9145-4d61-9695-42edadd6799b"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01LTQpfej6wjgnyzKhcCw6fv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01B3UrHow3aMrdCmoU83yUoa","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"ratekv","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260514T002109","overall_score":"3/6 Weak Reject","report":"---\ndream_id: dream_20260514T002109\ntier: best\noverall_score: 3/6 Weak Reject\nreviewer: R1 (Methods and Experiments)\ntimestamp: 2026-05-14\n---\n\n# R1 Review: Cardinality-Constrained MCKP for Per-Head Mixed-Precision KV Cache Quantization\n\n## Summary\n\nThe paper formulates per-head KV cache bit-width allocation as a cardinality-constrained Multiple-Choice Knapsack Problem (MCKP) over {2,4,8,16}-bit, where a cardinality constraint on the number of 2-bit heads regularizes compounding non-additivity that independent per-head profiling cannot capture. Experiments on three 7B-scale models show 22-28% PPL improvement over uniform 4-bit at 4x compression. The core insight that compounding effects create a sharp threshold, and that cardinality constraints can regularize this, is genuinely interesting. However, severe data integrity problems, missing critical baselines, absent statistical rigor, and no practical efficiency measurements substantially weaken the paper.\n\n## Strengths\n\n- **Clean formulation.** The MCKP formulation (Eqs. 1-4) is mathematically well-posed and maps naturally to the problem. The discrete action set {2,4,8,16} is hardware-aligned, giving a concrete advantage over continuous relaxations that require rounding. The solver is fast (<0.05s) and practically deployable.\n\n- **Compounding effect discovery is the paper's strongest contribution.** The elbow curve (Table 3 / Figure 1) provides the first quantitative evidence of non-additive error accumulation in multi-head KV cache quantization. The two-regime structure (additive slope 0.055 vs. compounding slope 1.29, 23x ratio) is a clean empirical finding that the community should know about regardless of the specific solver used.\n\n- **L0 auto-discovery experiment is convincing.** Table 7 demonstrates that the solver independently assigns 8-bit to all Layer 0 heads from profiling data alone, with 96.4% allocation agreement and only 0.05 PPL difference vs. the version with an explicit L0 exclusion constraint. This is the strongest piece of evidence that the MCKP formulation adds value beyond hand-crafted heuristics.\n\n- **Cross-model consistency.** Results on three architectures (Qwen/GQA 7:1, Llama/GQA 4:1, Mistral/GQA 4:1 with sliding window) showing consistent L0 dominance and 22-28% improvement strengthens the generalizability claim -- provided the data is real (see below).\n\n- **Writing quality.** The three-observation structure in the Introduction is effective. The paper is well-organized and easy to follow.\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n### Major\n\n**W1. Critical data integrity problem: paper numbers diverge from actual experimental records.**\n\nCross-referencing the paper's tables against the actual `compounding_sweep_results.csv` reveals systematic discrepancies:\n\n| Paper claim | Actual data | Issue |\n|---|---|---|\n| k=0: PPL 12.01 | PPL 15.4454 (= uniform 4-bit) | Paper reports 12.01 but k=0 means no 2-bit allowed; solver can only use {4,8,16} at budget 0.25, which forces uniform 4-bit. The actual data correctly shows 15.45. |\n| k=8: PPL 11.08 | PPL 11.2312 | Paper headline PPL is fabricated/projected. Actual measured PPL is 11.23. |\n| k=75: PPL 30.12 | PPL 35.348 | Paper value does not match records. |\n| k=112: PPL 36.92 | PPL 35.348 (identical to k=75) | Paper claims 36.92 but actual data shows k=75 and k=112 produce identical results (same allocation: 75 heads at 2-bit, solver self-limits). |\n| k=16: PPL 11.52 | PPL 11.809 | Off by ~0.3. |\n| k=24: PPL 13.41 | PPL 13.1448 | Off by ~0.3 in opposite direction. |\n| k=48: PPL 21.87 | PPL 22.8826 | Off by ~1.0. |\n\nThe headline result (28.3% improvement, PPL 11.08) does not match any actual measurement. The true improvement is 27% (PPL 11.23 vs 15.45), which is still substantial but the discrepancy is concerning. The systematic nature of these alterations (all shifted to make the story cleaner) raises serious concerns about the integrity of other reported numbers.\n\n**Severity: Major.** This alone would warrant rejection at a top venue. Every number in the paper must be traceable to actual experimental logs.\n\n**W2. Most multi-model and downstream results appear to be projections, not completed experiments.**\n\nThe project's experiment registry shows only Qwen2.5-7B experiments as completed (exp_c_constrained_mckp, exp_d_no_l0_exclusion, compounding_sweep). The Llama-3.1-8B, Mistral-7B-v0.3, RateQuant comparison, oracle comparison, cross-dataset, downstream task, and 72B scaling experiments are all listed as planned but not yet executed. The `imagined_experiments.yaml` file contains pre-written \"expected results\" that match the paper's tables digit-for-digit, confirming these are projections.\n\nThis means approximately 7 of 10 results tables in the paper report imagined rather than measured data. The cross-model consistency claim (the paper's strongest generalizability argument) rests entirely on projections.\n\n**Severity: Major.** A paper cannot report projected results as experimental findings.\n\n**W3. Missing critical baseline: L0-only heuristic.**\n\nThe paper's own records (memory.md) note that simply protecting Layer 0 at 8-bit while keeping everything else at 4-bit yields PPL ~10.62 on Qwen2.5-7B. This trivial heuristic (zero optimization, zero profiling beyond identifying L0) apparently *outperforms* the constrained MCKP's actual PPL of 11.23.\n\nIf confirmed, this is devastating for Claim 2: the entire MCKP optimization machinery (profiling 4N heads, solving the knapsack, tuning k) would be unnecessary. A single if-statement (\"if layer == 0: use 8-bit\") would beat it. The paper must include this comparison and explain why the more complex method is justified.\n\n**Severity: Major.** This could collapse the paper's central contribution.\n\n**W4. No quantization scheme specification.**\n\nThe paper never states which quantization method is used (RTN, GPTQ, AWQ, symmetric/asymmetric, per-channel/per-token, group size). This is not a minor omission: the sensitivity profile delta_{i,b} is entirely dependent on the quantization scheme. Different schemes can change which heads are sensitive and by how much. Results are not reproducible without this information.\n\n**Severity: Major.** Fundamental reproducibility issue.\n\n**W5. No ablation separating MCKP formulation value from cardinality constraint value.**\n\nThe paper conflates two contributions: (a) using MCKP instead of greedy/waterfilling for discrete allocation, and (b) adding a cardinality constraint. No experiment isolates their individual contributions:\n\n- What PPL does a simple greedy allocator with cardinality constraint achieve? If it matches MCKP, the formulation is incidental.\n- What PPL does RateQuant achieve when augmented with the same cardinality constraint? If it matches MCKP, the only contribution is the constraint idea, not the solver.\n- The paper shows unconstrained MCKP fails (PPL 36.92), but this only proves the constraint matters, not that MCKP itself matters.\n\n**Severity: Major.** Without this ablation, the reader cannot assess whether the MCKP formulation adds value over simpler approaches with the same constraint.\n\n### Minor\n\n**W6. No error bars or statistical significance.**\n\nAll results are single-run, single-seed. The profiling procedure uses a fixed calibration set (16 sequences x 512 tokens = 8K tokens). The sensitivity of results to calibration sample selection is unknown. At minimum, 3-5 seeds with different calibration subsets should be reported.\n\n**W7. No latency or throughput measurements.**\n\nKV cache quantization is motivated by practical inference efficiency, but the paper reports zero runtime metrics. Mixed-precision introduces kernel dispatch overhead (different GEMM kernels for different bit-widths within a single forward pass). Without wall-clock latency numbers, the practical value is unverifiable. It is entirely possible that the PPL improvement is offset by inference slowdown from non-uniform precision.\n\n**W8. Profiling cost not fully analyzed.**\n\nProfiling requires 4N forward passes (N heads x 4 bit-widths). For Qwen2.5-7B (N=112), this is 448 forward passes. For 72B (N=560), this is 2,240 forward passes (3.5 hours reported). The paper calls this a \"one-time cost\" but doesn't discuss:\n- Amortization: does the profile transfer across fine-tuned variants?\n- Sequence length sensitivity: the paper uses 512-token calibration but claims benefits for long-context. Do profiles change at 4K/8K/32K?\n- Batch effects: profiling one head at a time is sequential; can it be parallelized?\n\n**W9. Cardinality constraint applies only to 2-bit, not to other extreme assignments.**\n\nAt 8x compression (budget=0.125), many heads must be at 2-bit by necessity. The cardinality constraint on 2-bit becomes either vacuous (if k is large enough) or overly restrictive (if k is small). The paper's 8x result (PPL 28.67) is still far from practical -- what k was used? Is the same k=8 appropriate across compression ratios? The paper doesn't discuss k selection for different budgets.\n\n**W10. Oracle comparison methodology is flawed.**\n\nTable 6 evaluates MCKP vs. brute-force oracle on four independent layer groups. But the groups are evaluated independently -- each group's non-target layers remain at some fixed allocation. This does NOT measure whether the MCKP's full-model joint allocation is near-optimal, because:\n(a) Inter-group interactions (the paper's own compounding thesis) are not captured.\n(b) The oracle for a 28-head group has 4^28 ~ 7x10^16 configurations -- the paper claims \"sampled 10^6 + DP\" but doesn't explain how DP is applied to a non-standard knapsack with cardinality constraints.\n(c) Per-group optimality does not compose to whole-model optimality.\n\n**W11. RateQuant comparison is unfair.**\n\nThe RateQuant baseline is evaluated by rounding continuous waterfilling solutions to {2,4,8,16}. This is the weakest possible RateQuant variant. RateQuant's Algorithm 1 is a discrete greedy algorithm with proven optimality on consecutive integers. A fair comparison would:\n(a) Run RateQuant's greedy directly on {2,4,8,16} (without rounding from continuous).\n(b) Add cardinality constraint to RateQuant to isolate whether it's the constraint or the solver that matters.\nThe paper acknowledges RateQuant has a discrete greedy algorithm in the paper_framing.md but only evaluates the continuous variant.\n\n**W12. Downstream evaluation is too narrow.**\n\nMMLU and LongBench are evaluated only on Qwen2.5-7B, only at 4x compression, and only with the imagined (not actual) data. No generation quality evaluation (e.g., AlpacaEval, MT-Bench) is included. For KV cache quantization, generation coherence degradation is often more practically important than PPL or MMLU accuracy.\n\n**W13. The compounding threshold interpretation is over-fitted to a single model.**\n\nThe elbow at k~8-12 is observed on Qwen2.5-7B (112 heads). The paper claims this generalizes but provides no compounding sweep data for Llama or Mistral. The 72B \"estimated k~20-30\" is explicitly speculative. Without cross-model compounding sweeps, the claim that k can be determined by a \"small-scale compounding effect test\" is not validated.\n\n**W14. Missing visualization (plan_011).**\n\nThe experiment plan includes head sensitivity visualization (plan_011) which is missing from the paper. Figure 1 is a text-box placeholder, not an actual figure. For a paper whose core argument is about sensitivity heterogeneity, proper visualization is essential.\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. The actual compounding sweep data shows k=0 yields PPL 15.45 (= uniform 4-bit), but the paper reports 12.01. What accounts for this discrepancy? Is there a different k=0 configuration being used?\n\n2. What is the PPL when Layer 0 heads are assigned 8-bit and all other heads get uniform 4-bit? Your own records suggest ~10.62, which would outperform constrained MCKP at 11.23. How do you justify the optimization framework if this simple heuristic is superior?\n\n3. What quantization scheme is used (RTN, GPTQ, symmetric, asymmetric, group size)?\n\n4. Can you add cardinality constraints to RateQuant's greedy algorithm and report the resulting PPL? This would clarify whether the contribution is the constraint idea or the MCKP formulation.\n\n5. What k value is used at 2x and 8x compression? Is the same k=8 used across all budgets? If k must be re-tuned per budget, the profiling cost multiplies.\n\n6. Have you measured inference latency with mixed-precision KV cache? Modern inference engines (vLLM, TensorRT-LLM) may not efficiently support per-head mixed precision.\n\n7. The oracle comparison evaluates layer groups independently. Can you provide an estimate of the full-model oracle gap, accounting for inter-group interactions?\n\n8. How stable are the profiling deltas across different random calibration subsets? What is the coefficient of variation?\n\n## Experiment Coverage Assessment (vs. 13 planned experiments)\n\n| Plan ID | Status | In Paper? | Notes |\n|---------|--------|-----------|-------|\n| plan_001 (L0 auto-discovery) | Completed | Yes | Real data, convincing |\n| plan_002 (Llama pipeline) | Not started | Yes (projected) | Numbers match imagined_experiments.yaml exactly |\n| plan_003 (Mistral pipeline) | Not started | Yes (projected) | Numbers match imagined_experiments.yaml exactly |\n| plan_004 (Compounding sweep) | Completed | Yes (modified) | Real data exists but paper numbers don't match |\n| plan_005 (Budget sweep) | Not started | Yes (projected) | 2x and 8x results are projections |\n| plan_006 (Hi-res profiling) | Partially done | Yes (projected) | Some profiling done but paper numbers appear projected |\n| plan_007 (C4 cross-dataset) | Not started | Yes (projected) | All numbers projected |\n| plan_008 (RateQuant comparison) | Not started | Yes (projected) | Only weakest variant compared |\n| plan_009 (Oracle comparison) | Not started | Yes (projected) | Methodology has flaws even if run |\n| plan_010 (Cross-calibration) | Not started | Yes (projected) | Merged with plan_007 in paper |\n| plan_011 (Visualization) | Not started | No | Missing from paper entirely |\n| plan_012 (Downstream tasks) | Not started | Yes (projected) | Only MMLU + LongBench, not RateQuant's eval suite |\n| plan_013 (72B profiling) | Not started | Yes (projected) | Profiling-only, no eval |\n\n**Summary: 2 of 13 experiments are completed with real data. The paper presents ~12 experiments as if completed.**\n\n## Score\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)**\n\n**Confidence: 4/5 (High)**\n\n### Justification\n\nThe core insight (compounding effects create a sharp threshold regularizable by cardinality constraints) is genuine, interesting, and potentially impactful. The MCKP formulation is clean and practical. However, I cannot recommend acceptance for the following reasons:\n\n1. **Data integrity failure.** Paper numbers systematically diverge from actual experimental records, with all alterations favoring the narrative. This is the most serious concern.\n\n2. **~80% of reported results are projections.** Only Qwen2.5-7B has real data; the cross-model story (the paper's primary generalizability evidence) is entirely imagined.\n\n3. **A trivial baseline (L0-only heuristic) may outperform the proposed method.** If PPL ~10.62 < 11.23, the optimization framework provides negative value over a one-line heuristic.\n\n4. **No ablation separating the two contributions** (MCKP vs. cardinality constraint), making it impossible to assess what actually matters.\n\n5. **No practical efficiency validation** (latency, throughput), no error bars, no quantization scheme specification.\n\nThe research direction is promising and the compounding effect finding is worth publishing, but the current manuscript requires substantially more experimental work and data integrity corrections before it is suitable for a top venue. If the authors complete all planned experiments, add the missing baselines (L0-only, cardinality-on-greedy), fix data discrepancies, and add latency measurements, the paper could reach 4-5/6 territory.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":705,"cache_read_input_tokens":87788,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":705,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Cxbwokq1XMvJjn87kQykrf","session_id":"6cf1809e-9145-4d61-9695-42edadd6799b","uuid":"f975bfc5-fe13-460a-8fef-b8ad8f77bed1"}
